    "writingTasks": {
      "wt-960d0f8d-application": {
        "writingTaskId": "wt-960d0f8d-application",
        "groupId": "group-960d0f8d-writing-application",
        "taskType": "application",
        "title": "应用文写作",
        "instruction": "假定你是校报记者李华，在上周五的“校园开放日”(Open Day), 外教Chris承担了英语示范课教学。为准确报道该活动，请你给Chris写一封邮件，内容包括：\n(1)询问示范课的内容与亮点；\n(2)征求其出镜照片使用授权。\n(2)可适当增加细节，使内容充实、行文连贯。\nDear Chris,\nI’m Li Hua, a student reporter for our school newspaper.\nYours,\nLi Hua",
        "wordCountTarget": 80
      },
      "wt-960d0f8d-continuation": {
        "writingTaskId": "wt-960d0f8d-continuation",
        "groupId": "group-960d0f8d-writing-continuation",
        "taskType": "continuation",
        "title": "读后续写",
        "instruction": "My name is Shaquille O’Neal, but no, I am not the legendary basketball star everyone knows. My parents are big basketball fans, so they named me after him, sparking my love-hate relationship with the name. We share only our passion for basketball; he’s a tall, famous icon, while I’m a short boy who can barely dribble（运球）the ball without dropping it.\nEvery Saturday, the Central District's Boys and Girls Club sponsors league-play for all age groups. My age group, 9 to 11, is the biggest. For years, I was always the last picked. “He’s too small!” Louis, a team captain, would say coldly while my teammates laughed. I’d sit on the hard bench the whole game, then lie to my dad about having fun, my heart aching to do my part for the team instead of just watching from the sidelines.\nLast November, I got to play, thanks to Thanksgiving trips and flu season. I tried to make up for my poor skills by being really aggressive. I would always defend the biggest kid on the other team, and ended up with a bloody nose.\nThe Boys and Girls Club has a great nurse’s room. I know because I spent more time in there than on the court. The nurse was a Vietnamese lady, and because none of the kids could pronounce her name, they called her Skimmy. Skimmy didn’t know anything about basketball or any other American sports, but she was nice and had a pretty smile.\nBy my twelfth nosebleed, Skimmy was my friend. It was she who finally helped me become a better player. “Mr. Shaq,” she said one Saturday, “I watch you play. You always get hurt. Why do you play that way? Why don’t you take advantage of what you are? I watch you – you are fast. You are speedier than a meteor. Why don’t you get faster?”",
        "continuationStarters": [
          "Paragraph 1: What Skimmy had said made a lot of sense.",
          "Paragraph 2: The next Saturday, I stepped onto the court with a different plan."
        ],
        "materialText": "My name is Shaquille O’Neal, but no, I am not the legendary basketball star everyone knows. My parents are big basketball fans, so they named me after him, sparking my love-hate relationship with the name. We share only our passion for basketball; he’s a tall, famous icon, while I’m a short boy who can barely dribble（运球）the ball without dropping it.\n\nEvery Saturday, the Central District's Boys and Girls Club sponsors league-play for all age groups. My age group, 9 to 11, is the biggest. For years, I was always the last picked. “He’s too small!” Louis, a team captain, would say coldly while my teammates laughed. I’d sit on the hard bench the whole game, then lie to my dad about having fun, my heart aching to do my part for the team instead of just watching from the sidelines.\n\nLast November, I got to play, thanks to Thanksgiving trips and flu season. I tried to make up for my poor skills by being really aggressive. I would always defend the biggest kid on the other team, and ended up with a bloody nose.\n\nThe Boys and Girls Club has a great nurse’s room. I know because I spent more time in there than on the court. The nurse was a Vietnamese lady, and because none of the kids could pronounce her name, they called her Skimmy. Skimmy didn’t know anything about basketball or any other American sports, but she was nice and had a pretty smile.\n\nBy my twelfth nosebleed, Skimmy was my friend. It was she who finally helped me become a better player. “Mr. Shaq,” she said one Saturday, “I watch you play. You always get hurt. Why do you play that way? Why don’t you take advantage of what you are? I watch you – you are fast. You are speedier than a meteor. Why don’t you get faster?”",
        "wordCountTarget": 150
      }
    }
  },
